sec.p:
Among the seven domains of the KCL, the cognitive function domain was associated with a higher risk of all‐cause death [adjusted hazard ratio (HR): 1.654, 95% confidence interval (CI): 1.178–2.321, P = 0.004] and cardiovascular death (adjusted HR: 2.614, 95% CI: 1.590–4.298, P < 0.001) (Figure 
3
). The depression domain remained associated with a higher risk of HF rehospitalization (adjusted HR: 1.316, 95% CI: 1.007–1.719, P = 0.045) (Figure 
3
). Regarding the composite event of cardiovascular death and HF rehospitalization, the cognitive function domain was associated with a higher risk (adjusted HR: 1.300, 95% CI: 1.009–1.675, P = 0.042) (Figure 
3
). In sensitivity analysis, the total KCL score was significantly associated with the incidences of all‐cause death and cardiovascular death (adjusted HR: 1.819, 95% CI: 1.300–2.547, P < 0.001; adjusted HR: 2.272, 95% CI: 1.330–3.883, P = 0.005, respectively). The risk of all‐cause death increased with an increase in the total KCL score (Figure 
4
). The risk of cardiovascular death increased with an increase in the total KCL score up to 10, but the increase in the risk was smaller thereafter.
